---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const keplerCfg = {
  curves: [{ expr: 'x^1.5', label: 'T = a^1.5 (Kepler III)', color: '#4338ca', width: 2.2 }],
  points: [{
    points: [[0.39, 0.24], [0.72, 0.62], [1, 1], [1.52, 1.88], [5.2, 11.86], [9.54, 29.46], [19.19, 84.01], [30.07, 164.8]],
    label: 'the real planets', mode: 'dots', color: '#b42318',
  }],
  x: { min: 0, max: 32, label: 'orbit size a (AU)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 170, label: 'period T (years)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 360,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/foundational/solar-system-scale">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The solar system is mostly emptiness with a lighting problem. The natural yardstick is the
    <strong>astronomical unit (AU)</strong>: the Sun–Earth distance, 149.6 million km. Light, the
    fastest thing there is, needs <strong>8 minutes 20 seconds</strong> to cross it — so the Sun
    you see is always 8 minutes old. Neptune, thirty times farther, is over four light-hours away;
    Voyager 1, launched 1977, is more than a light-day out and still climbing.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="orbital period against distance — the curve is Kepler's third law; the dots are the actual planets" widget="plotter" config={keplerCfg} />

  <p>
    Hover the dots: every planet sits on the curve, from Mercury (88 days) to Neptune (165
    years). One power law, discovered 1619 from naked-eye data, survived Newton, relativity and
    5,000+ exoplanet discoveries.
  </p>

  <h2>Feels-like scale</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Shrink the Sun to a football</strong> (22 cm) and Earth becomes a 2 mm grain 23 metres away; Neptune orbits 700 metres out. The distances refuse to fit on any honest diagram.</li>
    <li><strong>Light is a slow coach here.</strong> Radio to Mars takes 3–22 minutes one way depending on orbits — which is why rovers must drive themselves.</li>
    <li><strong>The planets are specks.</strong> All eight worlds together hold under 0.2% of the solar system's mass; the Sun holds the other 99.8%+.</li>
    <li><strong>Beyond Neptune</strong> lies the Kuiper Belt (Pluto included) and, a thousand times farther, the Oort cloud's comets — the map still has blank edges.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NASA planetary fact sheets; Kepler, Harmonices Mundi (1619); JPL Voyager mission status.</p>
</TopicPage>
